.TH FACELOCK 1 "2026-09-05" "facelock" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
facelock \- Linux face authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B facelock
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B facelock
is a unified command-line tool for Linux face authentication. It handles
face enrollment, authentication, daemon management, camera preview,
benchmarking, and system setup. The companion PAM module
.BR pam_facelock (8)
connects to the facelock daemon or spawns
.B facelock auth
to perform face-based authentication for system services such as
.BR sudo (8)
and login managers.
.PP
All face recognition inference runs locally using ONNX models (SCRFD for
detection and ArcFace for embedding). No cloud services are contacted.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These are declared once and accepted by every subcommand, on either side of the
subcommand name \(em \fBfacelock \-c \fIpath\fB daemon\fR and
\fBfacelock daemon \-c \fIpath\fR are the same invocation.
.TP
.B \-c\fR, \fB\-\-config \fIpath\fR
Path to the config file. Takes precedence over
.BR FACELOCK_CONFIG .
.TP
.B \-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet
Suppress informational stdout and JSON payloads where supported; errors,
prompts, required notices and exit codes are unchanged. Human output from
status, bench, tpm, config, daemon restart, hyprlock, audit, list and devices
still prints. The JSON payloads of status, list and devices are suppressed;
the preview frame stream is not.
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose
Raise diagnostic verbosity on stderr, one level per repeat. A command starts at
.BR warn ,
so it prints warnings and errors and nothing quieter;
.B facelock daemon run
starts at info, because it writes to the journal.
.B RUST_LOG
outranks both. The level changes output volume only: exit codes and the
payloads on stdout are identical at every level.
.SH PRIVILEGE MODEL
Enrollment, model management, camera inspection/preview, status, the unified
benchmarks, TPM operations and audit access require root. Interactive CLI use
may offer to re-execute through sudo; service/script-oriented hard-root paths
such as \fBdaemon run\fR, \fBauth\fR's PAM use, and PAM writes must be invoked
with their required identity explicitly. \fBis-enrolled\fR,
\fBcapabilities\fR, \fBconfig show\fR and \fBpam status\fR are unprivileged.
The \fBhyprlock\fR group is user-owned and refuses root.
.PP
Under a non-default \fB\-\-config\fR, commands that would otherwise use the
packaged daemon operate directly because that daemon reads the fixed default
configuration. See each command below. The environment override is ignored by
all effective-UID-0 processes.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "facelock setup"
Download models and create directories. An interactive wizard guides initial
configuration.
.IP
Three rules govern the flags below. Supplying a value answers that question and
therefore replaces the corresponding prompt \(em there is no
\fB\-\-skip\-\fIx\fB\-prompt\fR family, because on the steps that change the
system "skip" would have to mean "apply the default", which is the opposite of
what an integrator asking to skip PAM wants. A \fB\-\-no\-\fIaction\fR flag
declines an action outright; declining is not the same as defaulting. And
\fBauto\fR means re-derive from the hardware, not "use the default" \(em
omitting the flag already gives the default.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-non\-interactive
Run in non-interactive mode (skip wizard). Performs the base setup only:
directories, model download and verification, encryption and path
permissions. No PAM, no systemd, and no enrollment unless explicitly
requested.
.TP
.B \-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes
Skip ordinary confirmation prompts. This does not authorize a sensitive PAM
edit. Also accepted as \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-sensitive
Explicitly authorize adding Facelock to \fBcommon\-auth\fR, \fBlogin\fR,
\fBpassword\-auth\fR, \fBpassword\-auth\-ac\fR, \fBsshd\fR,
\fBsystem\-auth\fR, \fBsystem\-auth\-ac\fR, or \fBsystem\-login\fR. This does
not suppress the confirmation prompt, requires \fB\-\-pam\fR, and conflicts
with \fB\-\-remove\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-pam
Install or manage PAM module configuration. On its own, configures one service
(see \fB\-\-service\fR) and touches nothing else.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pam
Decline PAM configuration entirely. No file under
.I /etc/pam.d
is read, backed up or written.
.TP
.B \-\-systemd
Validate the installed package/source-owned systemd and D-Bus assets, remove
only exact known legacy copies under
.IR /etc ,
using a no-replace quarantine transaction, reload and verify unit and activation
resolution, then enable the facelock daemon unit. Modified or linked legacy
copies are preserved and make the action fail. D-Bus policy fragments are
merged, so unrelated local policy is preserved and reported rather than called
ineffective. Immutable assets
under
.I /usr
are never written by setup.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-systemd
Decline systemd configuration. No unit files are written and
.BR systemctl (1)
is not invoked.
.TP
.B \-\-enroll
Enroll a face during setup, without the confirmation prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-enroll
Do not enroll a face during setup. The recognition test that depends on it is
skipped as well.
.TP
.B \-\-disable
Used with \fB\-\-systemd\fR: disable and stop systemd units instead. Requires
\fB\-\-systemd\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-service \fIname\fR
Used with \fB\-\-pam\fR: target PAM service (default: sudo). Requires
\fB\-\-pam\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-remove
Used with \fB\-\-pam\fR: remove the facelock PAM line instead of adding it.
Requires \fB\-\-pam\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-if\-present
Used with \fB\-\-pam\fR on add or remove: treat an absent service file as
success rather than an error. Read, parse and write failures remain fatal.
.TP
.B \-\-camera \fIpath\fR|\fBauto\fR
Camera device path, or \fBauto\fR to re-detect the IR camera from the attached
hardware. \fBauto\fR fails if zero or more than one IR-capable device is found;
an explicit path that does not exist is also an error.
.TP
.B \-\-models \fBstandard\fR|\fBbalanced\fR|\fBhigh\fR
Model quality preset. Selects the detector/embedder pair to configure and
download.
.TP
.B \-\-execution\-provider \fBcpu\fR|\fBcuda\fR|\fBrocm\fR|\fBopenvino\fR|\fBauto\fR
ONNX Runtime execution provider. \fBauto\fR asks the installed ONNX Runtime
which providers it was built with and selects cuda, rocm, openvino or cpu in
that order of preference, printing what it found. Availability is a property of
the ONNX Runtime build, not of the hardware: a CPU-only runtime reports no GPU
providers even on a machine with a GPU. If the runtime cannot be queried at
all, \fBauto\fR warns and selects cpu rather than failing the setup.
.TP
.B \-\-encryption \fBtpm\fR|\fBkeyfile\fR|\fBnone\fR|\fBauto\fR
Embedding encryption method. \fBauto\fR uses the TPM when a working TPM 2.0 is
present and a software keyfile otherwise. \fBtpm\fR fails rather than falling
back.
.RE
.IP
Flags compose. \fB\-\-pam\fR and/or \fB\-\-systemd\fR on their own perform just
that action; any flag that only makes sense while the base setup runs
(\fB\-\-non\-interactive\fR, a choice flag, or any of \fB\-\-no\-pam\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-systemd\fR, \fB\-\-enroll\fR, \fB\-\-no\-enroll\fR) forces the base
setup, and the requested actions run in addition to it. For an action pair, the
later flag on the command line wins.
.IP
\fB\-\-pam\fR is an alias onto
.B "facelock pam add"
and
.BR "facelock pam remove" ,
which is the primary spelling and the one that takes several services in one
process. Existing \fBsetup \-\-pam\fR invocations keep parsing; sensitive
additions use the same explicit \fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR authorization as
.BR "facelock pam add" .
\fB\-y\fR only suppresses the confirmation prompt.
.SS "facelock is-enrolled"
Report whether a user has a usable face enrollment. The exit status is the
contract: 0 if enrolled, 1 if not, 2 on error; stdout is the state word
.B enrolled
or
.BR not\-enrolled ,
after the fashion of
.BR "systemctl is\-active" .
See
.B EXIT STATUS
below. Its enrollment answer comes from the per-user marker, normally under
.IR /var/lib/facelock/enrolled/ ;
it first reads the selected config to locate the marker directory beside the
database, falling back to this default directory if config cannot be loaded.
It does not activate the daemon over D-Bus, open a camera, or read the face
database. No group is involved (ADR 010): the marker sits
under two 0711 root:root directories, so any local user can open its own
marker by name. A missing or unreadable marker (ENOENT or EACCES) is
reported
.B not\-enrolled
rather than as an error. The marker is a hint for deciding whether to offer a
face-auth affordance \(em PAM at authentication time remains authoritative.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output a JSON object with the keys \fBenrolled\fR (boolean), \fBmodels\fR
(integer) and \fBupdated\fR (ISO 8601 timestamp, or null) instead of the state
word. \fBmodels\fR is 0 and \fBupdated\fR is null when the user is not
enrolled. Does not change the exit status.
.TP
.B \-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet
Suppress stdout and report only via the exit status. Errors are still written
to stderr. This is the global flag (see GLOBAL OPTIONS), so
\fBfacelock \-\-quiet is\-enrolled\fR is the same invocation.
.RE
.SS "facelock capabilities"
Report what this build can do, as capability names \(em one per line on stdout.
Answers from the binary's own clap tree and compiled-in constants: it reads no
config file, activates no daemon and opens no camera, and so needs no root. It
is what replaces grepping \fB\-\-help\fR in a wrapper script, since help text
is not an API. Probe by name, never by version: a distro or git build can carry
a version that says nothing about what is in it.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit one JSON object with the keys \fBversion\fR (this binary's own version)
and \fBcapabilities\fR (a sorted, deduplicated array of names) instead of the
plain list.
.TP
.B \-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet
Suppress stdout, leaving the exit status as the whole answer. This is the
global flag (see GLOBAL OPTIONS).
.RE
.IP
Both forms exit 0; the command has no failure mode. A build that predates the
command answers by failing \(em unrecognized subcommand on stderr, exit 2 \(em
which a caller reads as "no capabilities at all". See
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.SS "facelock enroll"
Capture and store a face embedding for authentication.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username to enroll (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-l\fR, \fB\-\-label \fIlabel\fR
Label for this face model.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-setup\-check
Bypass the setup offer. Without it, an install whose setup\-complete marker is
missing is offered \fBfacelock setup\fR first. Accepting runs setup and returns
without a separate enrollment; setup's enrollment can be declined and does not
inherit this command's user or label. Declining setup also returns successfully
without enrolling. This flag is for a tree assembled by hand or by a
configuration manager, where the marker was never written but the models,
database and encryption key are in place; enrollment still fails on its own
terms if any of them is not.
.RE
.SS "facelock remove"
Remove the required decimal \fIMODEL_ID\fR shown by \fBfacelock list\fR.
The argument is an unsigned 32-bit integer; hexadecimal spellings are rejected.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt. Also accepted as \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR.
.RE
.SS "facelock clear"
Remove all face models for a user.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt. Also accepted as \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR.
.RE
.SS "facelock list"
List enrolled face models.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output as JSON.
.RE
.SS "facelock test"
Test face recognition against enrolled models for the current or specified user.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username (default: current user).
.RE
.IP
A zero exit status means the command completed, not that a scan ran or a face
matched. No usable enrollment and a completed non-match also return zero; read
the human result.
.SS "facelock preview"
Live camera preview with face detection overlay.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object per frame on stdout instead of opening the graphical
preview. This flag shipped as \fB\-\-text\-only\fR, which survives as a
hidden alias and keeps parsing; the per-frame payload is unchanged.
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
User to match faces against (defaults to current user).
.RE
.SS "facelock config"
Show or edit configuration. With no subcommand, shows.
.SS "facelock config show"
Print the config file path and contents, and whether the file parses. Reads a
0644 file and needs no root. Missing or invalid config is reported with exit 0;
an actual file-read failure returns an error.
.SS "facelock config edit"
Open the config file in \fB$EDITOR\fR (then \fB$VISUAL\fR, then nano/vi/vim),
validate on save, and request a restart when both old and new configurations
are valid and a setting in the restart list changed. Requires root. An invalid
saved file is retained with a warning and exit 0. Editor variables must name an
executable, not a command with arguments.
.SS "facelock status"
Check system status including daemon connectivity, enrolled faces, camera
availability, and model-file presence. Also reports whether the binary, models
and database needed by the PAM module's oneshot fallback exist, and whether
the target user's
.B is\-enrolled
marker agrees with the database. These presence checks do not prove a working
camera, valid model hashes, or successful authentication. Requires root. A check that cannot be
performed is reported as such; the report never substitutes a guessed value.
The \fBPAM services\fR line lists every service that carries the facelock
line, from the same scan
.B "facelock pam status \-\-all"
runs, and marks how many are a local override of a vendor file. It reads
\fBnone configured\fR only when every directory was read; when one could not
be, it reads \fBnot checked\fR and names the place on a line of its own.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object instead of the report, with a key per section
(\fBconfig\fR, \fBdaemon\fR, \fBoneshot_fallback\fR, \fBcamera\fR,
\fBmodels\fR, \fBexecution_provider\fR, \fBencryption\fR, \fBenrollment\fR,
\fBsecurity\fR, \fBnotifications\fR, \fBpam\fR). Each carries a \fBstate\fR of
\fBok\fR, \fBproblem\fR or \fBunknown\fR and, when it is not \fBok\fR, a
\fBreason\fR. A fact that could not be established is \fBunknown\fR with a
reason and no value \(em never a JSON \fBnull\fR and never a \fBfalse\fR. This
is the form to branch on instead of grepping the report. Exit codes are
unchanged: \fBstatus\fR exits 0 whenever it produced a report, and the verdicts
are in the document. See \fBfacelock status Semantics\fR in the contracts
document for the schema and its stability tier.
.RE
.SS "facelock devices"
List available camera devices.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the typed device array instead of the human listing. Format details are
present only on the direct backend; the D-Bus device type does not carry them.
.RE
.SS "facelock daemon"
Run or restart the persistent authentication daemon. With no subcommand, runs
it \(em the form every shipped service unit invokes.
.SS "facelock daemon run"
Run the daemon in the foreground. Registers on the D-Bus system bus as
\fBorg.facelock.Daemon\fR to service authentication and enrollment requests
from the PAM module and CLI.
.SS "facelock daemon restart"
Request a restart with systemctl. If it fails or cannot be launched, try a
D-Bus shutdown request so the service manager or later activation can restart
the daemon. The fallback result is unchecked: exit 0 does not prove a restart.
Requires root. The global config option does not change the target service or
the configuration it reads.
.IP
Takes \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR from GLOBAL OPTIONS above.
.SS "facelock auth"
One-shot authentication (used by the PAM module). Exits with code 0 on
success or non-zero on failure. The PAM module spawns
\fBfacelock auth \-\-user \fIname\fB \-\-config \fIpath\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-u\fR, \fB\-\-user \fIusername\fR
Username to authenticate. Required here and only here: the caller names the
subject, and it must never fall back to the process owner.
.RE
.IP
Takes \fB\-\-config\fR from GLOBAL OPTIONS above.
.SS "facelock bench"
Direct capture/inference measurements, requiring root. They do not exercise
PAM/daemon authentication policy, rate limiting or liveness. User selection is
SUDO_USER, then USER, then LOGNAME, then unknown; there is no user option.
Missing a timing target or face match does not itself make the command fail.
.SS "facelock bench cold-auth"
Measure cold auth latency (model load + first auth).
.SS "facelock bench warm-auth"
Measure warm auth latency (models already loaded).
.SS "facelock bench preview"
Measure frame capture and face processing (detection and embedding) latency.
.SS "facelock bench enrollment"
Measure enrollment time.
.SS "facelock bench model-load"
Measure ONNX model load time.
.SS "facelock bench calibrate"
Compare ten live captures with the selected user's templates and recommend the
threshold nearest a 90% pairwise match rate. Then sweep detector confidence on
another frame. This does not measure false-accept rates or update configuration.
.SS "facelock bench camera-reopen"
Measure what reopening the camera costs, reporting the open / STREAMON / warmup
split over \fIn\fR close\(->open\(->first\-usable\-frame cycles (default 5) as a
per\-phase median. Needs no enrolled face and loads no models. This is the total
.B device.camera_release_secs
trades LED\-on time against.
.TP
.B \-\-iterations \fIn\fR
Number of reopen cycles (default: 5).
.SS "facelock bench report"
Report environment details plus model-load, preview and enrollment-snapshot
timings. With enrolled faces it adds warm capture/match and approximate cold
auth (model reload and one capture on the already-open camera); otherwise
those rows are skipped. Requires a readable database. It does not run the
standalone cold-auth loop, calibrate or camera-reopen; its model-pack and build
labels are fixed text rather than detected metadata.
.SS "facelock tpm"
Manage the embedding encryption key and the TPM that can seal it. The group
owns the key's whole lifecycle, so \fBencrypt\fR, \fBdecrypt\fR and
\fBreseal\fR live here \(em the cipher is software AES-256-GCM either way, and
\fBencryption.method\fR decides only where the key lives.
.SS "facelock tpm status"
Report configured TPM-path and sealed-key presence, method and embedding counts.
Requires a readable database; it does not test whether the TPM can unseal.
.SS "facelock tpm seal-key"
Seal the existing AES key with TPM and set the encryption method to tpm.
Requires a plaintext keyfile, refuses an existing sealed blob, and retains the
plaintext keyfile as a recovery backup. Embeddings are unchanged.
.SS "facelock tpm unseal-key"
Unseal the AES key into a plaintext keyfile and set the method to keyfile.
Requires a sealed blob and refuses an existing plaintext keyfile, including
the backup retained by seal-key. The sealed blob and embeddings are unchanged.
.SS "facelock tpm unseal-check"
Read-only check that the sealed AES key currently unseals, verifying the PCR
policy is still satisfied. Writes nothing and exits non-zero on failure.
Diagnose the reported cause first: a missing/corrupt blob, wrong method, or
unavailable TPM is not repaired merely by resealing. A PCR-policy mismatch
may call for
.B "facelock tpm reseal"
when the key is recoverable through the current policy or a protected
plaintext backup.
.SS "facelock tpm pcr-baseline"
Display current PCR values for configured indices. This and the seal/unseal/
reseal operations require a build with TPM support and a usable TPM connection.
.SS "facelock tpm encrypt"
Encrypt all unencrypted embeddings in the database with AES-256-GCM.
\fB\-\-generate\-key\fR generates a new encryption key without encrypting
embeddings. Key generation refuses when encrypted templates exist or the
database cannot be checked; it does not change the configured method.
In-place encryption refuses when hard device binding is active.
.SS "facelock tpm decrypt"
Decrypt AES-256-GCM embeddings and legacy per-embedding TPM blobs into plaintext
rows. TPM blobs require TPM support. Does not change configuration or delete
keys. A later failure can leave earlier rows converted; device-bound encrypted
templates cannot be decrypted by this command.
.SS "facelock tpm reseal"
Re-seal the TPM AES key under the current PCR values \(em the recovery step
after a firmware or kernel change moves a measured PCR and the sealed key stops
unsealing. Requires root, and applies only when \fBencryption.method\fR is
\fBtpm\fR; under any other method it errors rather than quietly doing nothing.
It requires an existing sealed blob and prefers unsealing it, falling back to
the plaintext key backup if unsealing fails. Resealing before an update binds
the current PCRs, not the future values. Use
.B "facelock tpm unseal\-check"
to find out which of those applies.
.SS "facelock pam"
Manage the facelock line in
.I /etc/pam.d
service files. This command owns every write to that directory;
\fBsetup \-\-pam\fR is an alias onto it. \fB\-\-service\fR is repeatable on
all three verbs and defaults to \fBsudo\fR, so several services are configured
in one process under one root check. A name is looked up in
.I /etc/pam.d
first and
.I /usr/lib/pam.d
second \(em Linux\-PAM's own order, first hit wins \(em since packages ship
their configuration in the vendor directory; on current Arch \fBpolkit\fR
installs
.I /usr/lib/pam.d/polkit\-1
and no
.I /etc/pam.d
copy. Only
.I /etc/pam.d
is ever written to: a service found only in a vendor directory is copied there
first, with the facelock line already in it and a header naming what it was
forked from, and the package's file is left untouched. Configurable with
\fB[pam] config_dirs\fR.
.IP
Named removal deletes that local override only while its exact Facelock header,
payload, owner and mode still match the first existing vendor service in search
order. It moves the exact local inode to a no-replace transaction quarantine
and rechecks both names and the current vendor before deletion. Otherwise it
restores or keeps the drifted local copy and reports why. If the current vendor
source is absent, only a header naming a normalized configured candidate is
recognized, and only to report why the local copy is retained; the header path
is never opened.
.SS "facelock pam add"
Add the facelock line to one or more service files. Root. Accepts
\fB\-\-service \fIname\fR (repeatable), \fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
(alias \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR) to skip the per-file confirmation,
\fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR to also permit \fBcommon\-auth\fR,
\fBlogin\fR, \fBpassword\-auth\fR, \fBpassword\-auth\-ac\fR,
\fBsshd\fR, \fBsystem\-auth\fR, \fBsystem\-auth\-ac\fR and
\fBsystem\-login\fR, \fB\-\-if\-present\fR to treat a missing
service file as success, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR to print the resolved plan
without writing, and \fB\-\-json\fR (which implies \fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR,
since the prompt would block the pipeline reading the document).
\fB\-\-yes\fR never implies
\fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR: they are separate authorizations. Every service
is validated before any file is written. A service file that is a symlink out
of
.I /etc/pam.d
is refused rather than written through; in-directory links are refused too.
A file with more than one hard link is refused \(em a link count says another
name for it exists and not where. Before an in-place edit, a 0600 root:root
backup and its versioned provenance record are written under
.IR /var/lib/facelock/pam-backups/ .
.IP
On Debian and Ubuntu, a selected packaged \fBpam-auth-update\fR profile is
already one Facelock authentication path. Before planning a direct add,
Facelock verifies the fixed profile, saved selection and live Primary block
with bounded no-follow reads. It refuses a duplicate and instructs the
administrator to run
.BR "sudo pam-auth-update --disable facelock" ,
verify that a real correct password succeeds and a wrong password fails, then
retry the original Facelock command with all of its services and flags. Any
saved-selection/live-graph disagreement or untrusted state also refuses without
a PAM or backup write.
.SS "facelock pam remove"
Remove the facelock line. Root. Same flags as \fBadd\fR except
\fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR, which it does not offer \(em removal can only
take away a way to authenticate, so there is nothing to gate. It never prompts,
but accepts \fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR (alias
\fB\-\-no\-confirm\fR) for compatibility. Its other local options are
repeatable \fB\-\-service \fIname\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR,
\fB\-\-if\-present\fR, \fB\-\-keep\-backup\fR,
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, and \fB\-\-json\fR. It removes validated Facelock
backup state plus the exact legacy adjacent
\fI.facelock\-backup\fR name by default. \fB\-\-keep\-backup\fR preserves
both forms of rollback state. \fB\-\-all\fR conflicts with \fB\-\-service\fR
and scans the compiled system roots
.I /etc/pam.d
and
.I /usr/lib/pam.d
plus the detection-only generated root
.I /etc/authselect
instead of configured PAM directories. It recognizes exact pre-versioned
Facelock rules under conventional service names and current files authenticated
by strict provenance. Dot-prefixed and package or administrator artifact names
require provenance for that exact name or an exact current Facelock vendor-copy
header; unowned artifacts are preserved.
Customized rules, corrupt provenance, linked files and read-only-root references
block the whole operation without a write. A link is skipped only when its exact
target is the same service in a later compiled root that is scanned independently;
the link itself is never followed.
.IP
With \fB--dry-run\fR, an existing PAM backup directory is inspected
read-only and must already have its trusted owner and mode. The preview does
not repair or sync it, acquire its write lock, or run recovery.
.IP
The complete target set is backed up and journaled before the first PAM file
changes. Every replacement is identity-rechecked; a later failure or a final
scan that still finds an active reference restores every earlier original in
reverse order. A durable commit marker makes crash recovery finish validated
cleanup rather than roll the files back. Intent-only pre-publication recovery
requires the complete original identity and absent temp/binding; it is
restartable after rollback removes the checked temp, binding, then intent.
Pair cleanup resumes exact quarantine/unlink state and rejects partial
conflicts. This path
reads no Facelock config,
database, model, daemon, camera or inference state and is the cleanup command
used by package uninstallers while the binary and PAM module are still present.
.IP
The Debian package never silently disables a selected shared profile. Removal
probes it before the direct-edit dry run and aborts with package, module, PAM
and daemon state retained when it is selected or unsafe. Disable it explicitly
with \fBpam-auth-update\fR, verify correct and wrong password behavior, then
retry package removal. Older packages recorded no proof distinguishing an
automatic selection from an administrator choice, so automatic legacy
migration is intentionally unsupported. An unselected opt-in profile leaves
with the package only after direct cleanup commits; generated service handling
follows that cleanup. Ordinary removal stops the daemon while preserving enabled
state for reinstall, and purge retires that state.
.IP
An unchanged Facelock-created vendor override is also a cleanup target, including
the exact restart state where its module rule is already absent. Version 2 batch
journal and commit targets carry a required \fBdelete_override\fR boolean;
version 1 recovery requires that field to be absent. After the commit marker,
deletion rechecks the exact installed inode and the journaled header payload,
owner, mode and first existing fixed-root vendor service, plus the journal
backup identity and line-removed installed hash. Drift preserves the override,
and an already absent flagged target is an idempotent completed unlink.
.SS "facelock pam status"
Report whether services carry the line. Reads only and needs
.B no
root: this is the probe to branch on instead of grepping
.I /etc/pam.d
yourself. Offers \fB\-\-service\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR,
\fB\-\-if\-present\fR and
\fB\-\-json\fR, and neither
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR nor \fB\-\-allow\-sensitive\fR \(em there is no write
to preview or gate. \fB\-\-if\-present\fR means what it means on
\fBadd\fR and \fBremove\fR: an absent service file is reported and no longer
forces exit 2. \fB\-\-all\fR answers the other question \(em not "is this
name configured?" but "what is configured on this machine?" \(em by replacing
the service list (the two conflict) with every service in the resolved
directories whose file names \fBpam_facelock.so\fR, so a \fBpolkit\-1\fR
nobody thought to ask about is reported. It scans rather than reading a list of
what facelock has edited, since such a list drifts the moment
.I /etc/pam.d
is edited by hand. A directory that could not be listed is named and exits 2
rather than reported as holding nothing, and the "nothing is configured"
sentence is scoped to the directories that were read, so taking the human
answer with \fB2>/dev/null\fR cannot turn "I could not look" into "nothing is
there"; a directory that does not exist is neither case, because it
demonstrably holds no service files. Only regular files are read \(em a FIFO,
socket, device node or symlink to a directory is skipped rather than opened,
since reading a FIFO blocks until a writer appears \(em and an entry whose name
is not valid UTF\-8 is skipped and logged. A service whose file is a local copy
hiding a package's own of the same name reads
.B "(local override of \fIpath\fB)"
and carries a \fBshadows\fR key in \fB\-\-json\fR; that is a property of
the row, so it appears with \fB\-\-service\fR too, and on \fBadd\fR and
\fBremove\fR rows. The exit status is the
answer; see
.B EXIT STATUS
below.
.SS "facelock hyprlock"
Manage hyprlock lock-screen integration: the face glyph in
\fBplaceholder_text\fR and the \fBignore_empty_input = false\fR setting that
lets a bare Enter submit to PAM. Runs as your normal user and refuses to run as
root, since it edits
.I ~/.config/hypr/hyprlock.conf
by default, or \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hypr/hyprlock.conf\fR when set.
The config must already exist. Enable creates an adjacent
\fI.facelock-backup\fR if absent; disable leaves it in place. Wiring
.I /etc/pam.d/hyprlock
itself is a separate, root step \(em see \fBpam add\fR above.
.SS "facelock hyprlock enable"
Add the face icon and set \fBignore_empty_input = false\fR. \fB\-\-no\-icon\fR
sets only the functional flag, for a hyprlock font with no Nerd Font glyphs, and
leaves any existing icon alone.
.SS "facelock hyprlock disable"
Remove the face icon and set \fBignore_empty_input = true\fR unless
pam_fprintd.so is detected in /etc/pam.d/hyprlock or fingerprint:enabled is
true in the selected config. A fingerprint glyph alone does not preserve the
setting. Does not restore the backup or the setting's original value.
.SS "facelock hyprlock status"
Show the current hyprlock integration state.
.SS "facelock audit"
View the structured audit log. Requires root with no elevation prompt.
Disabled logging or a missing log prints an explanation and exits 0, including
in follow mode. Follow polls growth every 500 ms and does not reset its offset
after rotation or truncation; restart it after the log is replaced.
.RS
.TP
.B \-f\fR, \fB\-\-follow
Follow mode: watch for new log entries.
.TP
.B \-l\fR, \fB\-\-lines \fIcount\fR
Number of recent entries to show (default: 20).
.RE
.SS "facelock data"
Manage retained Facelock state.
.SS "facelock data purge"
Destroy retained Facelock state inside the compiled roots: enrolled
embeddings, the database and its sidecars, encryption keys and sealed
blobs, models, enrollment markers, audit logs, snapshots and upgrade
backups. Requires root
.I and
.BR \-\-allow\-destruction .
This is the sanctioned way to destroy biometric data before uninstalling;
package removal deliberately preserves it.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-destruction
Authorize irreversible destruction. Required. Nothing else implies it \(em
in particular
.B \-\-yes
suppresses the confirmation prompt and grants no authorization, and
.B \-\-json
does not either.
.TP
.B \-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt (also
.BR \-\-no\-confirm ).
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Classify the configured paths and remove nothing. Requires root but not
.BR \-\-allow\-destruction ,
and takes no lifecycle lease, so it does not stop the daemon. It does
.I not
open the compiled roots, so it reports nothing about what is stored in
them and gives no completeness verdict: in its document
.I complete
is null and
.I roots_examined
is false. An empty dry run means nothing was checked, not that nothing is
there.
.TP
.B \-\-leave\-activation\-barred
Keep the daemon stopped and D\-Bus activation barred after purging, for a
caller that uninstalls next. Face authentication stays off until the
named barrier file is removed and
.B systemctl daemon\-reload
runs, or until the machine reboots. Conflicts with
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the machine document instead of the human report. Branch on its
.I complete
field: it is false whenever a remnant, an external path, an
unclassifiable configuration, or an interrupt occurred.
.RE
.PP
Only
.IR /etc/facelock ,
.I /var/lib/facelock
and
.I /var/log/facelock
are traversed, and the roots themselves are never removed. Configuration
cannot add a root; a configured path outside them is reported as an
external remnant and left untouched. Symbolic links, hard\-linked files,
non\-regular objects, wrong ownership, group\- or world\-writable modes,
mount crossings, and anything beyond the depth or entry caps are refused
and reported rather than worked around.
.PP
Purge takes an exclusive lifecycle lock, bars D\-Bus activation and stops
the daemon for the duration, then restores exactly the prior state \(em on
success, on error, on panic, and on
.SM SIGINT ,
which stops the traversal at a deletion boundary before the daemon is
allowed back. Repeating a purge is safe.
.PP
The exit status is 0 whenever the purge ran and the daemon lifecycle was
restored, whatever the purge could not remove; the report, not the exit
code, is the answer. A run that retained anything says so and names every
remnant. If the lifecycle cannot be restored the full report is printed
first and the error returned after it, so a nonzero exit following a report
means the daemon needs attention, not that nothing happened. An interrupted
run prints no report at all: the signal handler restores the daemon and then
terminates the process while the command is still waiting on that restore.
Deletions already made stand, and repeating a purge is safe, so re-run it to
see what remains.
.B
Removing a name is not erasure:
filesystem deletion does not securely erase SSDs, snapshots, or backups.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Controls log verbosity via the tracing/env_filter crate. Example:
.BR RUST_LOG=debug .
Outranks both the built-in default and
.BR \-\-verbose ;
a value that cannot be parsed is reported at warn level and ignored.
.TP
.B FACELOCK_CONFIG
Override the configuration path only while the effective user is non-root.
Every effective-UID-0 process ignores it, including ordinary root CLI
commands. Use the explicit global \fB\-\-config\fR option when supported.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/facelock/config.toml
Main configuration file. Controls camera device, recognition thresholds,
daemon settings, security policy, and TPM options.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/
State directory. Permissions should be 711, owned by root:root:
traversable by every local user, listable by root only. Any user can open a
path it knows by name \(em its own enrollment marker, a model file \(em but
nobody can list the directory. The
.B facelock
group is retired (ADR 010); nothing under this directory is group-owned.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/facelock.db
SQLite database storing face embeddings. Permissions should be 600, owned
by root:root: the daemon reads it as root, and user-run PAM stacks request
authentication through the daemon rather than reading templates.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/models/
Directory containing ONNX model files (SCRFD and ArcFace). SHA256-verified
at load time. Public downloads, so permissions are 755 root:root with the
model files themselves 644; the 711 state directory above grants traversal to
a known name but no listing. Overridable via
.I daemon.model_dir
in the configuration file; an explicitly configured directory is honored
verbatim and is never rewritten.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/enrolled/
Enrollment markers read by
.BR "facelock is\-enrolled" .
Permissions should be 711, owned by root:root: traversable to a known
filename by any local user, listable by root only. No group is involved (ADR 010).
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/enrolled/<user>
One user's marker, 600 and owned by that user. Contains the model count and
the time it last changed. A hint for
.BR "facelock is\-enrolled" ,
never consulted during authentication.
.TP
.I /var/lib/facelock/pam-backups/
Root-only PAM rollback state. Backups are 0600 root:root with exact
\fI<service>.<seconds>\-<nine-digit-nanoseconds>\fR names and adjacent
versioned JSON provenance records; records contain no target path.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/org.facelock.Daemon.conf
D-Bus system bus policy file. Enforces a deny-all default: root may send the
whole interface and receive the signals, every local user may send only
.B Authenticate
for its own account, and nothing else is allowed (ADR 010). No group is
named.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/system-services/org.facelock.Daemon.service
D-Bus activation file for the facelock daemon service.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/security/pam_facelock.so
PAM module shared library.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (authentication passed, command completed).
.TP
.B non\-zero
Failure (authentication failed, error occurred).
.PP
.B facelock is\-enrolled
defines its exit status as its interface, so integrations branch on the status
rather than parsing stdout:
.TP
.B 0
The user has a usable enrollment.
.TP
.B 1
Not enrolled, or the enrollment is not usable. A missing marker and an
unreadable one are both reported this way.
.TP
.B 2
Error: an invalid
.BR \-\-user ,
an unparseable marker, or an I/O failure other than absence or access denial.
.PP
.B facelock pam status
uses the same 0/1/2 scale, for the same reason \(em it is a boolean query whose
exit status is the answer. Across several services the worst outcome wins:
.TP
.B 0
Every requested service carries the facelock line.
.TP
.B 1
At least one requested service exists without it.
.TP
.B 2
At least one is absent, unreadable, misnamed, hard\-linked, or symlinked out of
.IR /etc/pam.d .
With \fB\-\-if\-present\fR an absent service file no longer forces this, and
the status is decided by the services that do exist \(em absence only, so a
dangling or looping symlink is still 2.
.PP
.B "facelock pam status \-\-all"
uses the same scale about the machine rather than about a service list: 0 when
at least one service carries the line and every directory was read, 1 when
nothing carries it or an enumerated service has no line in the file Linux\-PAM
reads, and 2 when a directory could not be listed or an enumerated service
could not be answered for. \fB\-\-if\-present\fR does not change it \(em a
name reaches the report by having been found, so there is no absent case to
forgive, apart from a file deleted between the listing and the read.
.PP
.B facelock capabilities
always exits 0 \(em it has no failure mode. A build too old to have the command
exits 2 with clap's unrecognized-subcommand error on stderr and nothing on
stdout, which a caller reads as "no capabilities at all".
.PP
.B facelock auth
exits 0 when the face matched, 1 when a scan ran and did not match, 2 on an
error or when face authentication has no opinion (disabled, SSH session, lid
closed, storage failure, IR required, cancelled, not enrolled), 3 when the
user is rate limited, 4 when the attempt was suppressed (no enrolled models
with
.BR security.suppress_unknown ),
and 5 when every captured frame was dark. Codes 3\-5 let the PAM module give
these classes the same consequence in oneshot mode as over D\-Bus; a module
older than the split reads them as "no opinion" and falls through. The full
table and its compatibility invariants are in
.IR docs/contracts.md .
.SH SECURITY
The default configuration requires an IR camera
.RB ( security.require_ir=true ).
Frame variance checks reject sufficiently static input during authentication;
they do not stop video replay. Daemon and oneshot authentication limit
face-detected failures (5 per user per 60 seconds by default). Successful and
no-face attempts do not consume this budget. IPC uses the D-Bus system bus; the
daemon verifies caller UID via
.B GetConnectionUnixUser
before executing any method, and the system bus policy in
.I org.facelock.Daemon.conf
applies a deny-all default: the whole interface for root, and
.B Authenticate
for every local user's own account (ADR 010). Message size limits are
enforced by the D-Bus daemon.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_facelock (8),
.BR pam (8),
.BR sudo (8)
